 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "BURROW MUMP. A little hill of the Levels, topped with a ruined church. In a flood it stands up as an island, and the sheep file up to it like commuters."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 2,
   "text": "THE SOMERSET LEVELS. Flat as a pancake, drained by ditches called rhynes (say 'reens'). In winter the water comes back and the road becomes a lake. Welcome."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE SWEET TRACK. A wooden causeway built across the marsh in 3807 BC, one of the oldest engineered roads in the world. They had good timber and good timing."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "PEAT. Cut for fuel here for centuries, now left wet to store carbon and to keep the rarities happy. Please stay on the boards."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "WILLOW AND WETLANDS. Somerset withies (willow) have been made into baskets, coffins and sturdy fences for ages. Buy a basket. Hold it like a queen."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "somerset_levels_athelney",
   "title": "Alfred at Athelney",
   "text": "In 878, King Alfred of Wessex hid from the Danes in the marshes at Athelney, near here. Legend says he sheltered with a peasant woman and let her cakes burn while lost in thought. He went on to win the Battle of Edington, so the cakes were forgiven."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "somerset_levels_sedgemoor",
   "title": "Sedgemoor, 1685",
   "text": "On 6 July 1685 the Duke of Monmouth's rebels tried a night attack across the moor at Sedgemoor and were crushed by King James II's army. It is often called the last pitched battle fought on English soil. A deep rhyne in the dark did the rebels no favours."
  }
 ],
